company.

00:06:58.290 --> 00:07:01.870
So at the heart of
it is our technology.

00:07:01.870 --> 00:07:07.680
So just to give you an idea of
how significantly we are there

00:07:07.680 --> 00:07:11.050
on the business, these are
some of the partners whom

00:07:11.050 --> 00:07:15.810
with we work across Asia
and Indian subcontinent

00:07:15.810 --> 00:07:20.910
and Middle East and Africa
across all of our video games

00:07:20.910 --> 00:07:25.440
and Vuclip games.

00:07:25.440 --> 00:07:31.170
Now moving onto Vuclip's
journey toward serverless,

00:07:31.170 --> 00:07:32.280
so where we were--

00:07:32.280 --> 00:07:34.650
so this was our state
of affairs before we

00:07:34.650 --> 00:07:36.840
started adopting Google Cloud.

00:07:36.840 --> 00:07:42.630
So all of our applications which
were serving our main video

00:07:42.630 --> 00:07:44.820
applications are on prem.

00:07:44.820 --> 00:07:47.880
Now we had different
applications which were tailor

00:07:47.880 --> 00:07:51.540
made for different integrations,
for different telecom partners,

00:07:51.540 --> 00:07:54.610
for different telecom
regions, and all that.

00:07:54.610 --> 00:07:58.500
So all our data pipelines
were built on prem.

00:07:58.500 --> 00:08:03.620
So basically, we hired Kafka
for our data ingestion.

00:08:03.620 --> 00:08:06.990
So that was our data
ingestion engine.

00:08:06.990 --> 00:08:09.240
And then we used to
have Talend, which

00:08:09.240 --> 00:08:13.334
was our premium ETL engine.

00:08:13.334 --> 00:08:15.000
And then we had a
bunch of shell scripts

00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:17.220
which we used to do
data transformation,

00:08:17.220 --> 00:08:20.030
and data was getting loaded
to the GreenPlum, which

00:08:20.030 --> 00:08:25.350
was, again, our primary data
warehouse, which was on prem

00:08:25.350 --> 00:08:27.540
and then we had a
bunch of Tableau,

00:08:27.540 --> 00:08:30.090
servers basically a
couple of Tableau servers,

00:08:30.090 --> 00:08:34.590
from where we served
our visualizations.

00:08:34.590 --> 00:08:36.690
Whatever pain points were there?

00:08:36.690 --> 00:08:39.690
For every new integration
or every new business

00:08:39.690 --> 00:08:42.950
that we entered into,
for our insights

00:08:42.950 --> 00:08:45.840
to be ahead of that,
what we needed to do

00:08:45.840 --> 00:08:49.030
was every time, evaluation
of existing capacity,

00:08:49.030 --> 00:08:50.640
how much traffic
this new integration

00:08:50.640 --> 00:08:52.290
is going to bring up.

00:08:52.290 --> 00:08:54.300
We have to see whether
our Kafka cluster is

00:08:54.300 --> 00:08:56.890
going to serve that up.

00:08:56.890 --> 00:09:00.720
Then we had to plan for
the manual storage capacity

00:09:00.720 --> 00:09:02.240
for our GreenPlum database.

00:09:02.240 --> 00:09:04.130
How is our data
going to increase?

00:09:04.130 --> 00:09:06.310
So we had to plan
for that as well.

00:09:06.310 --> 00:09:10.950
And then at the times, we had to
reshuffle all of the schedules

00:09:10.950 --> 00:09:13.530
based on different
geographies because we

00:09:13.530 --> 00:09:16.620
have to distribute load
which is whether its

00:09:16.620 --> 00:09:18.630
on the GreenPlum for
exhibiting queries

00:09:18.630 --> 00:09:20.880
or whether it's Talend for
running different jobs.

00:09:20.880 --> 00:09:24.020
So we have to every time
juggle here and there

00:09:24.020 --> 00:09:27.680
for our capacity optimization.

00:09:31.380 --> 00:09:34.710
So how did we move onto
the Google Cloud Platform.

00:09:34.710 --> 00:09:36.680
So basically, the first
approach that we took

00:09:36.680 --> 00:09:42.860
was, you know, finding out how
does our data plane look like?

00:09:42.860 --> 00:09:45.440
How are the patterns
of ingestion?

00:09:45.440 --> 00:09:49.990
So this is where we came up with
our data architecture patterns.

00:09:49.990 --> 00:09:54.245
And so basically,
we identified what

00:09:54.245 --> 00:09:57.320
are the different components
which are crucial for us

00:09:57.320 --> 00:09:58.554
on the Google Cloud.

00:09:58.554 --> 00:10:00.470
So if you see the first
three components which

00:10:00.470 --> 00:10:03.020
identify the storage,
so different storage,

00:10:03.020 --> 00:10:05.300
for different types of
storage, the components

00:10:05.300 --> 00:10:08.660
that we identified was
Cloud Storage, BigQuery,

00:10:08.660 --> 00:10:09.370
and DataStore.

00:10:09.370 --> 00:10:12.710
So these primarily served over
all the persistent storage

00:10:12.710 --> 00:10:14.040
requirements.

00:10:14.040 --> 00:10:15.890
The next one was Pubsub.

00:10:15.890 --> 00:10:19.190
This is, again, storage,
but this served our need

00:10:19.190 --> 00:10:20.510
for the transient storage.

00:10:20.510 --> 00:10:24.240
So basically we replaced
Kafka with the Pubsub.

00:10:24.240 --> 00:10:26.174
Then came the processing.

00:10:26.174 --> 00:10:27.840
What are the different
processing things

00:10:27.840 --> 00:10:30.450
that we need to use, you know?

00:10:30.450 --> 00:10:33.270
That is basically our data
engineering technologies.

00:10:33.270 --> 00:10:36.420
So we had Dataflow,
Cloud Functions, and then

00:10:36.420 --> 00:10:41.250
App Engine that was identified
as our processing horsepowers.

00:10:41.250 --> 00:10:42.630
And then we had Compute Engine.

00:10:42.630 --> 00:10:46.630
We used this for orchestration
of all of our pipelines.

00:10:46.630 --> 00:10:51.630
So this is how we zeroed
on our components, which

00:10:51.630 --> 00:10:54.050
we are going to use for
our different architecture

00:10:54.050 --> 00:10:55.080
patterns.

00:10:55.080 --> 00:10:58.170
If you look at all of these,
except for Compute Engine,

00:10:58.170 --> 00:10:59.880
all are serverless.

00:10:59.880 --> 00:11:03.610
So basically, they are
offered as, you know, mainly

00:11:03.610 --> 00:11:05.440
as platform as a service.

00:11:05.440 --> 00:11:07.960
So you don't have to worry
about the infrastructure aligned

00:11:07.960 --> 00:11:09.580
behind it.

00:11:09.580 --> 00:11:14.430
Now let me go into the
different architecture patterns

00:11:14.430 --> 00:11:15.550
that we have.

00:11:15.550 --> 00:11:17.890
So the first is on
the top, we have

00:11:17.890 --> 00:11:20.230
our simple, real-time
streaming use case.

00:11:20.230 --> 00:11:24.060
So all the data which comes
in from the outside sources

00:11:24.060 --> 00:11:25.440
lands on the Pubsub.

00:11:25.440 --> 00:11:29.310
Then through Dataflow
streaming pipeline,

00:11:29.310 --> 00:11:33.060
it goes to our BigQuery,
which is operational store.

00:11:33.060 --> 00:11:35.940
And then the next use
case, which is basically

00:11:35.940 --> 00:11:40.950
our low volume and low compute
processing-- so all of our data

00:11:40.950 --> 00:11:44.190
this in the batch mode arrives
at the Google Cloud Storage.

00:11:44.190 --> 00:11:49.000
For low volume, it gets
processed via the Google Cloud

00:11:49.000 --> 00:11:49.800
Functions.

00:11:49.800 --> 00:11:52.320
And then data goes again
onto our operational store,

00:11:52.320 --> 00:11:53.970
which is BigQuery.

00:11:53.970 --> 00:11:57.850
Now for the high volume and
high compute requirements,

00:11:57.850 --> 00:12:00.690
data which is landing onto
the Google Cloud Storage

00:12:00.690 --> 00:12:04.260
gets processed by the Dataflow,
which is in the batch mode.

00:12:04.260 --> 00:12:07.440
That processes data and
goes onto the BigQuery's

00:12:07.440 --> 00:12:09.780
operational store.

00:12:09.780 --> 00:12:12.690
Now we also have
different indications

00:12:12.690 --> 00:12:14.700
which are basically
API-based integration

00:12:14.700 --> 00:12:18.500
with internal systems as
well as external systems.

00:12:18.500 --> 00:12:21.090
So for this, we use
App Engine, which

00:12:21.090 --> 00:12:23.970
is integrated with different
APIs which pull data

00:12:23.970 --> 00:12:28.408
from different sources and
then push it onto the BigQuery.

00:12:28.408 --> 00:12:34.290
And then last of our case,
which is basically data that we

00:12:34.290 --> 00:12:37.080
have-- so data coming
from external sources,

00:12:37.080 --> 00:12:42.390
internal systems, and then data
massaging with our own data

00:12:42.390 --> 00:12:44.940
and then putting that data
or publishing that data

00:12:44.940 --> 00:12:46.570
to the external systems--

00:12:46.570 --> 00:12:48.030
so that was, again, a use case.

00:12:48.030 --> 00:12:51.690
So on this, we get that data
onto the Google Cloud Storage.

00:12:51.690 --> 00:12:55.400
Dataflow amalgamates that
data via out operational data

00:12:55.400 --> 00:12:58.680
store and then puts
it onto the Pubsub.

00:12:58.680 --> 00:13:00.840
From the Pubsub,
different Cloud functions

00:13:00.840 --> 00:13:02.640
get triggered and
sends that data

00:13:02.640 --> 00:13:04.630
to their respective
destinations.

00:13:04.630 --> 00:13:07.170
So this is our kind
of all of our library

00:13:07.170 --> 00:13:09.120
through which we just
keep on extending

00:13:09.120 --> 00:13:11.220
and all our use
case gets classified

00:13:11.220 --> 00:13:13.140
in all these major buckets.

00:13:16.590 --> 00:13:19.740
Now that we had sorted
out our architecture

00:13:19.740 --> 00:13:21.810
and how we are
going to attack it,

00:13:21.810 --> 00:13:24.930
so basically, that
was our stage of what

00:13:24.930 --> 00:13:28.230
we call as proof of technology.

00:13:28.230 --> 00:13:29.970
Now the other problem
that we needed

00:13:29.970 --> 00:13:34.830
to solve was how do we make
data available to everyone

00:13:34.830 --> 00:13:36.660
with access and security?

00:13:36.660 --> 00:13:40.210
So data consumption
was our next strategy.

00:13:40.210 --> 00:13:44.050
So the way we did
was all our data

00:13:44.050 --> 00:13:46.160
was bucketed into two things.

00:13:46.160 --> 00:13:50.020
So we had Data Storage project
and Data Access projects.

00:13:50.020 --> 00:13:51.640
So the architecture
pattern that we

00:13:51.640 --> 00:13:54.610
saw on the operational
Data Store and Data

00:13:54.610 --> 00:13:55.870
Warehouse, which was there--

00:13:59.060 --> 00:14:02.200
so on the right most.

00:14:02.200 --> 00:14:04.870
So those two are the
Data Storage projects.

00:14:04.870 --> 00:14:10.060
So only the data gets
ingested over there and access

00:14:10.060 --> 00:14:11.800
is not enabled
from these places.

00:14:14.550 --> 00:14:16.830
So this is how we
bifurcated that.

00:14:16.830 --> 00:14:21.710
Now the way it was managed was
all the data which is there

00:14:21.710 --> 00:14:26.790
onto the storage project gets
accessed via the Data Access

00:14:26.790 --> 00:14:27.510
project.

00:14:27.510 --> 00:14:32.449
The way we do it is we
create different user groups.

00:14:32.449 --> 00:14:33.990
So on the different
user groups, they

00:14:33.990 --> 00:14:35.730
have different
kinds of privileges.

00:14:35.730 --> 00:14:40.460
So all the users who have
access to certain kinds of data,

00:14:40.460 --> 00:14:42.550
they will be assigned
to certain groups.

00:14:42.550 --> 00:14:45.370
And that is how they will
be able to access that data.

00:14:45.370 --> 00:14:48.440
Now the way we created these
BigQuery views was they

00:14:48.440 --> 00:14:51.540
are restricted with
timelines and the amount

00:14:51.540 --> 00:14:53.440
of columns which are exposed.

00:14:53.440 --> 00:14:56.160
For example, the
views would be created

00:14:56.160 --> 00:15:00.371
with the restriction of 30
day data, 60 day data, 90 data

00:15:00.371 --> 00:15:00.870
data.

00:15:00.870 --> 00:15:02.710
For example, the
more raw data it

00:15:02.710 --> 00:15:06.420
is, we limit it to
the 30 day, 60 day.

00:15:06.420 --> 00:15:10.410
And the more aggregated data
is, we try to make it available

00:15:10.410 --> 00:15:11.920
as much as we can.

00:15:11.920 --> 00:15:14.220
So what this does
is it enables us

00:15:14.220 --> 00:15:18.420
to access, provide access
data securely, to all

00:15:18.420 --> 00:15:20.250
of the business users, analysts.

00:15:20.250 --> 00:15:25.110
What it also does
is basically, we

00:15:25.110 --> 00:15:28.440
can connect all of our
business intelligence tools

00:15:28.440 --> 00:15:31.620
to this Data Access project.

00:15:31.620 --> 00:15:34.330
So we are never touching
our Data Storage project.

00:15:34.330 --> 00:15:38.700
The other thing what it does is
it doesn't duplicate the data.

00:15:38.700 --> 00:15:41.880
Our data is still residing
on the Data Storage project

00:15:41.880 --> 00:15:45.150
and we still have all the
data available via Data Access

00:15:45.150 --> 00:15:46.950
project.

00:15:46.950 --> 00:15:49.620
And on top of it,
limiting the amount

00:15:49.620 --> 00:15:52.590
of data which is there
onto the Data Access

00:15:52.590 --> 00:15:55.680
project, what it does
is it controls the cost

00:15:55.680 --> 00:15:57.280
onto the BigQuery.

00:15:57.280 --> 00:15:58.860
So the querying
cost gets reduced

00:15:58.860 --> 00:16:01.290
because the number
of business users

00:16:01.290 --> 00:16:03.960
who are querying that,
they may accidentally

00:16:03.960 --> 00:16:05.160
access all the data.

00:16:05.160 --> 00:16:06.480
But now you are limiting them.

00:16:06.480 --> 00:16:10.170
Now who accesses the
data and what he accesses

00:16:10.170 --> 00:16:12.360
and what amount of data
he accesses-- so you

00:16:12.360 --> 00:16:15.940
have pretty much control that.

00:16:15.940 --> 00:16:18.960
So now that we had sorted
out architecture and data

00:16:18.960 --> 00:16:22.290
access, the next thing,
we were onto our journey

00:16:22.290 --> 00:16:25.500
towards migration, the
actual project, the migrating

00:16:25.500 --> 00:16:28.980
from in-house on prem
to the Google Cloud.

00:16:28.980 --> 00:16:33.040
The first set like I talked
was the proof of technology

00:16:33.040 --> 00:16:34.670
and then actual development.

00:16:34.670 --> 00:16:38.490
In this, we got our
data and then, you know,

00:16:38.490 --> 00:16:42.960
started developing the pieces
that we explained earlier.

00:16:42.960 --> 00:16:46.560
In the first phase, our systems
were live on prem as is,

00:16:46.560 --> 00:16:48.760
and we started our
development and testing,

00:16:48.760 --> 00:16:50.970
and we tested with
all the use cases.

00:16:50.970 --> 00:16:54.510
And then next important
step in the migration

00:16:54.510 --> 00:16:57.600
was the data migration.

00:16:57.600 --> 00:16:59.610
Whatever the historical
data we had--

00:16:59.610 --> 00:17:03.380
so that was our longest
portion which took time.

00:17:03.380 --> 00:17:09.300
So we moved all of our
historical data onto the GCP

00:17:09.300 --> 00:17:12.030
as well as our
reporting system still

00:17:12.030 --> 00:17:15.210
was live with our
old on-prem systems.

00:17:15.210 --> 00:17:18.990
In the next phase, we
did the parallel run.

00:17:18.990 --> 00:17:22.650
So basically, streaming data
was going onto both the systems.

00:17:22.650 --> 00:17:24.920
So we had historical
data, and streaming data

00:17:24.920 --> 00:17:29.070
was now going to both the
systems on prem as well as GCP.

00:17:29.070 --> 00:17:31.985
Now once the data was
going on to the GCP,

00:17:31.985 --> 00:17:35.920
we enabled reporting for a
few pilot things from the GCP.

00:17:35.920 --> 00:17:39.330
So all of our reporting
for the business

00:17:39.330 --> 00:17:41.400
was now for the pilot projects.

00:17:41.400 --> 00:17:45.210
We expose them through the GCP.

00:17:45.210 --> 00:17:47.330
Once we started out
issues with the pilots

00:17:47.330 --> 00:17:52.200
and we were good to go, then
we actually moved onto--

00:17:52.200 --> 00:17:55.080
all of the products
onto the GCP.

00:17:55.080 --> 00:18:01.170
There, we disconnected reporting
from the on prem system.

00:18:01.170 --> 00:18:03.660
We had streaming
data which was still

00:18:03.660 --> 00:18:06.780
going to the on prem that
was just as a backup,

00:18:06.780 --> 00:18:08.110
still going to the on prem.

00:18:08.110 --> 00:18:10.500
And then our streaming data
was going onto the GCP.

00:18:10.500 --> 00:18:14.820
All of our reporting
was done from the GCP.

00:18:14.820 --> 00:18:19.110
And then in the final stage,
we cut off our on-prem systems.

00:18:19.110 --> 00:18:21.240
And as this moment,
we have everything

00:18:21.240 --> 00:18:22.275
running onto the GCP.

00:18:25.780 --> 00:18:32.730
OK, so we have made our journey
reaching till, you know, GCP.

00:18:32.730 --> 00:18:33.750
Everything is there.

00:18:33.750 --> 00:18:36.130
But it is not stagnant.

00:18:36.130 --> 00:18:39.540
Every day, there will be changes
which will keep on coming in.

00:18:39.540 --> 00:18:41.490
You have to change
your pipelines.

00:18:41.490 --> 00:18:43.900
You have to change your
logics and all that.

00:18:43.900 --> 00:18:46.130
So what is the most
important thing, then?

00:18:46.130 --> 00:18:50.730
So you need to have continuous
deployment processes and test

00:18:50.730 --> 00:18:53.400
processes which will
take care of things

00:18:53.400 --> 00:18:57.360
like compute creation,
packages, create installations,

00:18:57.360 --> 00:19:01.890
whether its an SDK upgrade or
some library in Java, Python.

00:19:01.890 --> 00:19:05.230
You need to take care of
storage creations, the buckets,

00:19:05.230 --> 00:19:07.210
folders, the management of that.

00:19:07.210 --> 00:19:10.320
The BigQuery tables, view
creation, their [INAUDIBLE],,

00:19:10.320 --> 00:19:11.760
anything--

00:19:11.760 --> 00:19:13.640
Pubsub and Dataflow pipelines.

00:19:13.640 --> 00:19:16.740
So all of these things you
need to take care of in order

00:19:16.740 --> 00:19:21.090
to be able to run your
systems into the GCP.

00:19:21.090 --> 00:19:23.760
Once you migrated, that is
not the end of the journey,

00:19:23.760 --> 00:19:24.390
actually.

00:19:24.390 --> 00:19:25.950
Your journey begins from there.

00:19:29.800 --> 00:19:33.220
Now having said
that, let's actually

00:19:33.220 --> 00:19:36.466
go into a few of our
use cases in detail

00:19:36.466 --> 00:19:37.840
to get the gist
of it-- you know,

00:19:37.840 --> 00:19:40.270
what are the complex
things we are solving.

00:19:40.270 --> 00:19:42.790
To go into these
use cases, I would

00:19:42.790 --> 00:19:43.930
like to call upon Koushik.

00:19:46.570 --> 00:19:50.593
[APPLAUSE]

00:19:50.593 --> 00:19:52.093
KOUSHIK KULKARNI:
Thank you, Chetan,

00:19:52.093 --> 00:19:55.240
and thank you Google
Next Team and Rochelle

00:19:55.240 --> 00:20:01.250
for allowing us to present what
we have done so far on GCP.

00:20:01.250 --> 00:20:04.124
So what is available,
you have already

00:20:04.124 --> 00:20:05.290
seen that what is available.

00:20:05.290 --> 00:20:09.730
Now what we can do with all of
these tools and technologies

00:20:09.730 --> 00:20:10.870
that we have--

00:20:10.870 --> 00:20:14.560
the first use case came
right from our migration from

00:20:14.560 --> 00:20:17.080
the in-house system
to Google Cloud--

00:20:17.080 --> 00:20:18.430
data marts and dashboards.

00:20:18.430 --> 00:20:19.300
Everyone has those.

00:20:19.300 --> 00:20:20.530
We also had some.

00:20:20.530 --> 00:20:25.510
We had to keep them running
when we migrated the data.

00:20:25.510 --> 00:20:29.590
So initially, we started working
with the application logs,

00:20:29.590 --> 00:20:31.430
plain application logs.

00:20:31.430 --> 00:20:36.410
The streaming logs were
ingested through Pubsub

00:20:36.410 --> 00:20:39.100
by the streaming
Dataflow pipelines

00:20:39.100 --> 00:20:40.580
that you see over there.

00:20:40.580 --> 00:20:43.160
And then the batch data was
first extracted onto GCS

00:20:43.160 --> 00:20:46.315
and then loaded onto BigQuery.

00:20:46.315 --> 00:20:48.490
One of the fantastic
features that we used,

00:20:48.490 --> 00:20:50.660
that was flexible schemas.

00:20:50.660 --> 00:20:53.300
So that basically
allowed us to adapt

00:20:53.300 --> 00:20:58.590
to the upstream logs event model
changes in almost real time.

00:20:58.590 --> 00:21:02.440
And then we used the
aggregate data marts

00:21:02.440 --> 00:21:05.140
to create-- basically, we
use the SQL, BigQuery SQL,

00:21:05.140 --> 00:21:07.410
to create the
aggregated data marts.

00:21:07.410 --> 00:21:11.020
These were then used by the
dashboards and visualizations.

00:21:11.020 --> 00:21:13.450
Initially, we started
with Tableau on prem

00:21:13.450 --> 00:21:16.900
because we were not sure of the
[INAUDIBLE] platform stability.

00:21:16.900 --> 00:21:21.640
Once it got stabilized, we
migrated Tableau to online.

00:21:21.640 --> 00:21:25.300
Then we had some partner reports
and third party data extracts

00:21:25.300 --> 00:21:28.150
which actually were enabled
by the aggregate data

00:21:28.150 --> 00:21:29.980
marts themselves.

00:21:29.980 --> 00:21:33.790
Then along the line came
YouTube data requirements.

00:21:33.790 --> 00:21:37.300
So we are a media company
as you all know now.

00:21:37.300 --> 00:21:40.100
So we have our YouTube channels.

00:21:40.100 --> 00:21:43.150
From there, we wanted to
get our engagement data.

00:21:43.150 --> 00:21:46.040
So the BigQuery transfer
services fitted right in.

00:21:46.040 --> 00:21:48.340
I mean, with a few
clicks, we were

00:21:48.340 --> 00:21:50.700
able to enable the
Dataflows and start

00:21:50.700 --> 00:21:53.480
measuring the user engagements.

00:21:53.480 --> 00:21:55.760
Cost-- cost is definitely
an important factor

00:21:55.760 --> 00:21:57.170
that you should not ignore.

00:21:57.170 --> 00:22:02.030
So all our GCP billing
data, that is the--

00:22:02.030 --> 00:22:05.570
which all jobs are getting
triggered, which all users are

00:22:05.570 --> 00:22:09.200
using these services, what
are the number of bytes

00:22:09.200 --> 00:22:11.990
they are processing, what
is the chargeable cost--

00:22:11.990 --> 00:22:15.350
everything is exported now
to the BigQuery itself.

00:22:15.350 --> 00:22:17.770
So you can create dashboards
and alerts based on that.

00:22:21.900 --> 00:22:24.930
The next use case is around
user journey management.

00:22:24.930 --> 00:22:28.230
Now this actually originated
from the need of our product

00:22:28.230 --> 00:22:33.760
team to tweak the application
experience for each user.

00:22:33.760 --> 00:22:37.050
So this earlier-- we had
some of the ideas that

00:22:37.050 --> 00:22:39.020
were identified that
we should probably

00:22:39.020 --> 00:22:42.810
offer these kind of offers
to a certain region.

00:22:42.810 --> 00:22:46.500
Or we should change the way,
you know, the sign up flow

00:22:46.500 --> 00:22:48.720
works in certain regions.

00:22:48.720 --> 00:22:51.570
But then for each
of those changes,

00:22:51.570 --> 00:22:54.930
we needed longer
application chain cycles.

00:22:54.930 --> 00:22:58.290
To tackle that, we created this
kind of user journey management

00:22:58.290 --> 00:22:59.830
system.

00:22:59.830 --> 00:23:04.410
The first one in the system
is basically the user data--

00:23:04.410 --> 00:23:05.730
user master data.

00:23:05.730 --> 00:23:08.890
So all of that obligations
actually generate logs.

00:23:08.890 --> 00:23:10.050
We ingest those.

00:23:10.050 --> 00:23:13.190
We create the central
user view using Dataflow.

00:23:13.190 --> 00:23:17.710
And we push those user
changes onto Pubsub.

00:23:17.710 --> 00:23:20.090
To create the
segments themselves,

00:23:20.090 --> 00:23:22.820
like user segmentation
and then attaching

00:23:22.820 --> 00:23:27.410
certain policies which are how
the application should behave

00:23:27.410 --> 00:23:29.810
at a certain given
customization point,

00:23:29.810 --> 00:23:32.390
we created a user interface.

00:23:32.390 --> 00:23:35.650
That was hosted on
Google Kubernetes Engine.

00:23:35.650 --> 00:23:39.640
The backend APIs for that were
provided by Cloud Functions.

00:23:39.640 --> 00:23:44.860
The backend data store was
chosen to be DataStore.

00:23:44.860 --> 00:23:48.520
Once the configuration is
done from this particular UI,

00:23:48.520 --> 00:23:51.130
the messages flow through
Pubsub to Dataflow

00:23:51.130 --> 00:23:54.310
again where, in a
distributed manner,

00:23:54.310 --> 00:23:57.070
it decouples or it
basically resolves

00:23:57.070 --> 00:24:00.040
the segment and their
priorities for each user

00:24:00.040 --> 00:24:02.370
and creates user
level configuration.

00:24:02.370 --> 00:24:04.630
And that then is
pushed again to Pubsub.

00:24:04.630 --> 00:24:06.920
From there, it is
available to any consumer.

00:24:06.920 --> 00:24:10.540
So we have various kinds of
applications that we run.

00:24:10.540 --> 00:24:12.310
We have mobile browser.

00:24:12.310 --> 00:24:16.660
We have application Android IOS
and whatnot and also backend.

00:24:16.660 --> 00:24:21.050
Some of the functionality is
governed by the backend itself.

00:24:21.050 --> 00:24:23.920
So all of that can then
subscribe to the cross cloud

00:24:23.920 --> 00:24:26.060
configurable system.

00:24:26.060 --> 00:24:30.880
And what worked here really
well for us is the Dataflow

00:24:30.880 --> 00:24:32.450
windowing basically--

00:24:32.450 --> 00:24:35.380
so I think we have seen some of
the windowing in other sessions

00:24:35.380 --> 00:24:36.460
also.

00:24:36.460 --> 00:24:40.360
The session level windows,
late arrival of data,

00:24:40.360 --> 00:24:43.120
custom triggering based
on certain panes, that

00:24:43.120 --> 00:24:46.250
really worked well
for us in this case.

00:24:46.250 --> 00:24:52.580
The Pubsub subscriptions,
we created basically a push

00:24:52.580 --> 00:24:55.870
subscription to AWS systems
also from where it is

00:24:55.870 --> 00:24:59.680
served to the AWS applications.

00:24:59.680 --> 00:25:04.830
And Pubsub handles those push
subscriptions with retries

00:25:04.830 --> 00:25:06.160
with exponential backups also.

00:25:06.160 --> 00:25:09.370
So a lot of boilerplate code has
really been eliminated for us.

00:25:12.150 --> 00:25:15.580
The next use case was around
event-based optimizations.

00:25:15.580 --> 00:25:19.200
So as you see, there are some
attribution related events

00:25:19.200 --> 00:25:22.470
that we are ingesting
using App Engine.

00:25:22.470 --> 00:25:26.370
That is actually being fed to
the reference data preparation

00:25:26.370 --> 00:25:28.800
which is handled in Dataflow.

00:25:28.800 --> 00:25:31.560
The persistent data, the
reference data for each user

00:25:31.560 --> 00:25:35.850
is hosted in DataStore.

00:25:35.850 --> 00:25:38.700
And then that is actually used
by another Dataflow process

00:25:38.700 --> 00:25:42.200
which handles batch and
stream events to prepare

00:25:42.200 --> 00:25:44.040
or enrich those events.

00:25:44.040 --> 00:25:49.440
And again, they are queued
for dispatch in Pubsub.

00:25:49.440 --> 00:25:54.690
From there, the Cloud Functions
have custom event preparation

00:25:54.690 --> 00:25:58.060
and dispatch, which is then sent
to AppsFlyer and MoreEngage.

00:25:58.060 --> 00:26:03.200
So we are basically trying to
reward the ad networks which

00:26:03.200 --> 00:26:06.540
are providing high quality
users as well as we are trying

00:26:06.540 --> 00:26:09.600
to engage more with
the users if they are

00:26:09.600 --> 00:26:11.520
showing some kind of behavior.

00:26:11.520 --> 00:26:13.530
So all of that was
actually enabled

00:26:13.530 --> 00:26:16.840
by this particular application.

00:26:16.840 --> 00:26:19.620
And advanced analytics--
who can forget that?

00:26:19.620 --> 00:26:21.390
So this is one of
the simple use cases

00:26:21.390 --> 00:26:26.250
that we did where we wanted it
to understand how our customer

00:26:26.250 --> 00:26:28.560
or what actually they
are talking about us

00:26:28.560 --> 00:26:31.450
across various countries.

00:26:31.450 --> 00:26:33.570
So we have presence
across a lot of countries,

00:26:33.570 --> 00:26:37.050
and that basically comes
with the understanding

00:26:37.050 --> 00:26:39.540
that there are various
languages that are

00:26:39.540 --> 00:26:42.790
being used in these countries.

00:26:42.790 --> 00:26:45.600
So we started-- how
we went about this?

00:26:45.600 --> 00:26:49.612
We started with taking the
customer data loading into GCS.

00:26:49.612 --> 00:26:52.650
We used [INAUDIBLE]
Cloud Functions

00:26:52.650 --> 00:26:56.430
to load this data into
BigQuery whenever that came in.

00:26:56.430 --> 00:26:59.160
And from there, we
used translation API

00:26:59.160 --> 00:27:03.180
to translate all of those
various different languages,

00:27:03.180 --> 00:27:06.300
feedback in different
languages, into English.

00:27:06.300 --> 00:27:07.800
From there, we use
[INAUDIBLE] based

00:27:07.800 --> 00:27:10.590
classification and
natural language

00:27:10.590 --> 00:27:12.990
API for sentiment analysis.

00:27:12.990 --> 00:27:15.540
And we basically created
some sort of NPS,

00:27:15.540 --> 00:27:18.000
if you will, and
again visualized it

00:27:18.000 --> 00:27:21.510
in dashboard in Tableau online.

00:27:21.510 --> 00:27:24.810
So the use case
actually highlights

00:27:24.810 --> 00:27:29.100
usage of ML algorithms
that are out of the box,

00:27:29.100 --> 00:27:32.910
and they actually come with
a very good architecture

00:27:32.910 --> 00:27:37.840
that you can use without
doing a lot of coding.

00:27:37.840 --> 00:27:39.930
They are very well-tested,
so you don't really

00:27:39.930 --> 00:27:44.320
have to worry about the
retraining and everything.

00:27:44.320 --> 00:27:46.820
And you can prototype
quickly, right?

00:27:46.820 --> 00:27:49.580
So you can deliver
the business use case

00:27:49.580 --> 00:27:52.280
while it is still relevant.

00:27:52.280 --> 00:27:56.390
So in general,
what we understand

00:27:56.390 --> 00:28:00.200
from all of these use cases
is that we can focus--

00:28:00.200 --> 00:28:02.480
using all of these
cloud components,

00:28:02.480 --> 00:28:06.740
we can focus on our
architecture and focus

00:28:06.740 --> 00:28:11.360
on our business use case
rather than the boilerplate.

00:28:11.360 --> 00:28:13.370
We do have to plan for capacity.

00:28:13.370 --> 00:28:16.220
We do have to watch for
cost, even those are actually

00:28:16.220 --> 00:28:18.680
provided by the Cloud platform.

00:28:18.680 --> 00:28:21.080
